WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.600
Telescopes revealed the most distant galaxy supercluster seed yet, showing how the cosmic web first took shape. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 188. An international team spotted the earliest known seed of a galaxy supercluster through the Odin Survey. Narrowband imaging isolated Lyman alpha light from young galaxies inside the structure. The object already held substantial mass when the universe was under 2 billion years old.

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:44.280
The result lines up with models that placed the first large knots of the cosmic web at high redshift. From the universe's largest structures forming early, we turn to how individual galaxies and their central engines sometimes grow at very different rates.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:56.440
Observations show some supermassive black holes pull in mass faster than their host galaxies build stars. Large supplies of cold gas lose angular momentum and stream straight to the center.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:09.040
In the strongest cases, the light from this accretion outshines the galaxy's total starlight by several times. The trigger that lets so much gas reach the black hole without first forming stars remains unclear.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:19.260
While black holes and galaxies show mismatched growth, closer to home, new telescope pairings are revealing unexpected patterns among the solar system's smallest members.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:31.260
The James Webb Space Telescope and Hubble work together for the first time on faint transneptunian objects. The combined data turned up fewer small bodies than earlier models expected.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:42.040
Colors stayed consistent from the smallest detected objects up through larger ones. The pattern points to formation conditions that stayed stable across the early outer solar system.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:56.880
From the outer solar system's preserved building blocks, we move to a dramatic new image of a stellar nursery right next door. Hubble photographed Nebula N-44 and its central cavity measuring 210 by 140 light years.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:06.500
Winds and supernovae from young massive stars carved out the void. Thick shells of gas and dust now border the cavity and host fresh star formation.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:14.640
While Hubble shows star formation in action nearby, another spacecraft just proved a new navigation technique at an asteroid.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:25.700
Hyabusa 2 carried out the first laser-ranging test during a July flyby of an asteroid. The instrument returned precise distance readings as the spacecraft passed the target.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:32.040
The successful run shows the method works at asteroid range and widens options for future navigation.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:41.380
Precision navigation advances also matter for commercial satellite fleets, which brings us to a busy broadband constellation reaching completion.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:54.400
SpaceX launched the final satellites that completed the SES-03BM Power Network in medium-earth orbit. The added craft brought the constellation to its planned number of operational satellites.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:03:06.020
While operators finish one constellation, another company is scaling up a fully reusable rocket design. Stokespace released details on the block 2 version of its nova rocket.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.940
Larger tanks and engines raise payload capacity, while the vehicle keeps full reusability.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:23.040
Simplified manufacturing steps are meant to support more frequent flights. Reusable launch progress on Earth contrasts with long-term questions about how our own moon came to be.

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:34.620
Updated simulations test whether a giant impact could produce the Earth moon isotope match within hours. Temperature and material strength during the collisions and out as the main variables.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:46.260
Moon formation models reminded us that planetary systems can lose moons as well as gain them. From lost moons, we shift to water delivery in young planetary systems still under construction.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:57.240
Astronomers at Lund University found signs of exo-comments around the young star PDS-70. The comets appear to carry water ice inward toward the inner disk.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:10.260
The system serves as a nearby example of the processes that may have supplied early Earth with water. Water delivery in other systems raises questions about what happens when giant impacts strip oceans from icy moons.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:23.400
Simulations at Southwest Research Institute show large collisions remove or thin subsurface oceans on icy moons. The models indicate impacts destroy existing water rather than create new oceans.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:34.360
The findings apply to outer solar system moons that went through early heavy bombardment. Ocean loss on moons leads naturally to efforts to make fuel from planetary atmospheres.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:45.360
Researchers at the University of Mississippi are testing catalysts that turn Martian carbon dioxide into methane. The work aims at lower byproduct levels under simulated Martian conditions.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:56.320
The goal is institute propellant production for future return missions. Propellant ideas on Mars connect to a rover still climbing and exploring the real Martian surface.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:09.360
Curiosity passed the 1 kilometer elevation mark above its landing site during saws 49.95 through 5.001. The team noted the rover has grown into a capable mountaineer across its long mission.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:20.000
Laird rock studies continue as the rover keeps ascending Mount Sharp. While one rover keeps climbing, another satellite keeps moving in Earth orbit with no announced reason.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:31.840
The high list two satellite began its third major longitude shift since 2024. Tracking shows the spacecraft continues to change position, though no official explanation has been given.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:45.020
Fast radio bursts offer another way to map matter across vast distances. Dispersion measures from bursts traveling billions of light years reveal gas density in galactic halos and the intergalactic medium.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:06:00.900
Researchers are checking whether the signals can separate feedback effects from dark matter distributions. The Odin surveys detection of the most distant galaxy protosupercluster relied on narrow band imaging that isolates Lyman alpha emission from forming galaxies.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:12.340
This technique picks out structures still assembling when the universe was less than 2 billion years old. The finds strengthens the picture that the cosmic webs largest knots began growing very early.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:23.560
It also supplies a benchmark for simulations that must now reproduce such massive over densities at high redshift. How many more of these seeds remain hidden at even greater distances?

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:34.320
Picture a city whose downtown core gains new residents 10 times faster than the surrounding suburbs expand. That is the scale difference astronomers now measure in some galaxies.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:49.460
The central black hole can double its mass while the galaxies stars increase by only a modest fraction. Large reservoirs of cold gas lose angular momentum and funnel inward, feeding the black hole at rates far above what star formation consumes.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:59.920
These episodes can last tens of millions of years, enough time for the black hole to reach billions of solar masses while the galaxies stellar disc grows more slowly.

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:13.980
In the most extreme cases, the black hole's accretion luminosity exceeds the entire galaxy's starlight output by factors of several. Yet the trigger that lets so much gas reach the center without first turning into stars remains unknown.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:28.320
We can map the final mass ratios, but nobody yet knows why the gas sometimes bypasses star formation so efficiently. Before we go, keep an eye on upcoming results from the James Webb Space Telescope on early galaxy clusters.

